<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/magnetism-and-induction">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Magnetism is what electric charge does when it <strong>moves</strong>. A current makes a
    magnetic field (Ørsted, 1820 — compass needles twitch near wires); a magnetic field pushes
    moving charges sideways; and a <em>changing</em> magnetic field creates voltage from nothing
    (Faraday, 1831). Those three facts generate every motor, transformer and power station on
    Earth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Faraday's law, step by step — the chain from moving magnet to the grid" widget="equationStep" config={faradayCfg} />

  <h2>The connections that electrified the world</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Motor:</strong> current through a field feels a force F = BIL (sideways) — arrange the geometry and the loop spins. Electricity → motion.</li>
    <li><strong>Generator:</strong> the same machine reversed — motion through a field induces current. Motion → electricity. Every power plant except solar is a Faraday machine: coal, wind, hydro, nuclear all differ only in what spins the coil.</li>
    <li><strong>Transformer:</strong> alternating current in one coil changes flux through a second; the voltage scales with the turns ratio. This is why the grid transmits at high voltage (less I²R loss) and steps it down for your sockets.</li>
    <li><strong>Maxwell's completion:</strong> changing electric fields also make magnetic fields (1861) — closing the loop between the two forces. Light is the travelling knot of the two (see the advanced ladder).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ørsted (1820); Faraday, Experimental Researches (1831); Maxwell, "On Physical Lines of Force" (1861).</p>
</TopicPage>
